      ],
      "source": "https://indyencyclopedia.org/fall-creek-place/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2000, the area was nicknamed “Dodge City” because of a high crime rate before a major revitalization effort began.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fallcreekplace.com/about-fall-creek-place",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A $15 million public investment in streets, sidewalks, lighting, utilities, and trees helped turn abandoned lots into a mixed‑income community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fallcreekplace.com/about-fall-creek-place",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The City of Indianapolis and King Park Development Corporation partnered on the project, with Indiana Landmarks overseeing historic preservation.",
